course: dictionary
lesson: 2563
title: Delicious and strong words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Delicious» means tasting or smelling very good. You can use it for food, drinks and smells, like «smells delicious» or «absolutely delicious». Look at the sentence around the word: the clues tell you what it describes.
explain: «Fry a piece of meat and the outside turns brown and smells delicious.» The words brown, fry and meat tell you the food is cooking, so delicious describes a smell that is very good. Try swapping in a simpler word like nice. It still fits, but delicious is stronger.
explain: Delicious can also describe a feeling that is very pleasant. «A delicious feeling of being warm» is not about food at all. It means the warm feeling is very pleasant, the way a good meal makes you feel.
explain: Look out for a trap: «They look delicious, but some of the most dangerous plants in Europe have the prettiest berries.» Here but warns you that looking delicious can be wrong. Never judge something as safe or tasty from how it looks.
fact: Delicious means tasting or smelling very good.
fact: Delicious can describe anything very pleasant, such as «a delicious feeling of being warm».
fact: «Absolutely delicious» and «smells delicious» are natural ways to use the word.
fact: Delicious is a stronger word than «nice» or «tasty», so writers use it to show how good something really is.
fact: Some plants look delicious but are dangerous, so never eat wild plants or berries unless an adult who knows them says they are safe.
exercise: «Fry a piece of meat and the outside turns brown and smells delicious.» What does delicious mean here? | *Tasting or smelling very good | Hard and cold | Very large | Noisy and loud
exercise: «They look delicious, but some of the most dangerous plants in Europe have the prettiest berries.» What does the word but tell you? | The berries are all safe to eat | The author is hungry | *Looking delicious can be misleading | The plants are very large
exercise: Which word could replace delicious in «a delicious feeling of being warm»? | Noisy | *Pleasant | Cold | Sharp
test: Ahmed says «delicious» is just a fancy word for «nice». Why might a writer choose delicious instead? | *It is stronger and tells the reader exactly how good something is | It is only used for drinks | It means the food is cold | Shorter words are always better, so it saves space
test: Which phrase is a natural way to use delicious? | The delicious ran away quickly | Delicious, a window or a chair | *Smells delicious | Delicious the table
exam: Alice tastes the bottle and finds it delicious. What does this tell you? | Alice thinks it is poison | Alice is too tired to eat | *Alice thinks it tastes very good | Alice is angry with the bottle
